<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chittor Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chittorgarh, Chittorgarh Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chittor Fort, or Chittorgarh, is a hill fort above the Berach River in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and one of the largest forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the chief seat of the Guhila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> rulers of Mewar until 1568 and fell three times, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> in 1303, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a> in 1535 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1568, each fall remembered for the jauhar of its women. Its monuments include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijay-stambha">Vijay Stambha</a>, and it is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chittor-fort.jpg" alt="Chittor Fort" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssjoshi111 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chittorgarh_fort.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
